  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nEarly morning elevated convection to our northwest continued its\nevolution into an MCC that brought rain to a good portion of the\narea this morning. Lingering areas of light to moderate rain and\nembedded thunder continue in the cooler air mass left in its\nwake early this afternoon. Rain chances will decrease through the\nafternoon, but the moisture axis remains aligned with northwest\nflow aloft. Additional showers and thunderstorms could develop\nalong this zone again late tonight into Tuesday morning. There\nisn't a widespread risk for severe thunderstorms with this next\nround, but gusty winds and heavy rain are always possible with \nany stronger storms.\n\nEqually as exciting as the rainfall is the lower temperatures.\nThe KJAN temperature approaching 2 PM is 78 degrees. Thinning\nclouds in the wake of rain may allow a little rebound in\ntemperatures, but the threat for dangerous heat today was washed\nout. Tomorrow is uncertain as well, given the possibility for rain\nin the morning, but will advertise \"Limited\" threat area for\ndangerous heat tomorrow mainly our northeast Louisiana parishes,\nsoutheast Arkansas counties, and portions of Mississippi west of\nI-55 and in the Pine Belt. In these areas, heat index could\napproach 105 degrees in the afternoon pending rain development.\n\nRain chances continue Wednesday into Friday associated with\ndisturbances through the northwest flow aloft, and then it appears\nthat the ridge to our west will break down and translate eastward\nagain along the Gulf Coast region. A relatively drier stretch then\nheaded into early next week. Once the ridge pivots to our east\nearly next week, rain chances should transition to focus on sea\nbreeze development and air mass thunderstorms. /NF/",
